  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Peraton obligated $2.2B in FY2026 to date, from 313 awards with an average award size of $6.9M. Peraton's obligations are up 2.9% year over year. Peraton moved from $466M in FY21 to $2.2B in FY26, with intermediate totals of $1.1B in FY22, $1.7B in FY23, $2B in FY24, and $2.1B in FY25. Peraton sold most to the General Services Administration, which obligated $701M, followed by the Department of Defense at $404M and the Department of Transportation at $275M. Peraton's largest markets are Computer Systems Design Services at $982M and Engineering Services at $460M, with additional activity in Custom Computer Programming Services at $228M and several telecommunications and computer-related services. Peraton also recorded obligations to NASA at $216M, the Department of Commerce at $163M, and the Department of Health and Human Services at $158M. Current-year totals are partial and represent fiscal year to date activity.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How have Peraton's obligations changed over recent fiscal years?",
        "body": "Peraton's obligations grew from $466M in FY21 to $2.2B in FY26. Peraton's year-by-year totals are $466M in FY21, $1.1B in FY22, $1.7B in FY23, $2B in FY24, $2.1B in FY25, and $2.2B in FY26. Peraton's obligations are up 2.9% year over year."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and markets account for most of Peraton's federal work?",
        "body": "The General Services Administration is Peraton's largest buyer at $701M, followed by the Department of Defense at $404M and the Department of Transportation at $275M. Computer Systems Design Services is Peraton's largest market at $982M, followed by Engineering Services at $460M and Custom Computer Programming Services at $228M. Peraton also has notable obligations with NASA at $216M, the Department of Commerce at $163M, and the Department of Health and Human Services at $158M."
      }
    ]
  },
